Subject: [PATCH] DRM: do not sum relative movement across multiple mouse
 fds

The stock code accumulates relative movement additively
("CORE.Input.Mouse.currentPosition.x += event.value") - correct for a
single mouse fd, but once the 0003 patch started polling multiple
mouse-classified fds per call, any additional device that ALSO reports
movement for the same physical motion (a second, redundant HID
interface of the same physical mouse, or a genuinely separate second
pointing device both actually present) gets its deltas summed into the
SAME shared position, moving the cursor further/faster than the real
physical motion.

Confirmed via a real regression report: double-click in the file
browser (main.cpp, its own position+timing based detection - see that
file's own comments on why it doesn't rely on raygui's built-in
active-item toggling) stopped recognizing genuine double-clicks after
the 0003 patch - the two clicks of a double-click landing at
inconsistently different accumulated positions fails that detection's
own "same spot" (6px) check.

Only one fd's relative movement is now allowed to actually update the
cursor position per PollMouseEvents() call - whichever fd reports
movement first in that call "wins" for the whole call, others'
REL_X/REL_Y deltas are ignored. Button/wheel/key events are
deliberately NOT gated the same way - those are boolean/discrete
assignments, not accumulative, so duplicate reports from multiple fds
aren't the same class of problem. EV_ABS (absolute-position devices,
e.g. touchscreens) is also left untouched - it directly assigns rather
than accumulates, so a second device there simply overwrites rather
than compounds, which isn't a regression from the 0003 patch's own
"poll all, no additional ordering guarantee" design.

Verified: the core guard logic (only the first fd per call updates
position, a duplicate-reporting second fd is ignored, whichever fd
reports first "wins" regardless of its position in the poll order)
tested in isolation against 3 scenarios - all passing. Full file (with
0001-0004 already applied) compiles cleanly, and the app links and
starts without crashing against the resulting library.

Upstream-Status: Pending

diff --git a/src/platforms/rcore_drm.c b/src/platforms/rcore_drm.c
index 93edd6d5..2dcf0fff 100644
--- a/src/platforms/rcore_drm.c
+++ b/src/platforms/rcore_drm.c
@@ -2511,6 +2511,28 @@ static void PollMouseEvents(void)
     }
     if (pollFdCount == 0) return;
 
+    // PATCH: only let ONE fd's relative movement (REL_X/REL_Y) actually
+    // update the cursor position per call, not every polled fd summed
+    // together. Found via a real regression report: double-click in
+    // the file browser (main.cpp, its own position+timing based
+    // detection, see that file's comments) stopped recognizing genuine
+    // double-clicks after this multi-mouse-fd patch. Root cause: the
+    // stock code below accumulates relative movement ADDITIVELY
+    // ("CORE.Input.Mouse.currentPosition.x += event.value") - fine for
+    // a single fd, but once multiple mouse-classified fds are polled
+    // in the same call (this patch's whole point), any device that
+    // ALSO reports movement for the same physical motion - a second,
+    // redundant HID interface of the same physical mouse, or a
+    // genuinely separate second pointing device both actually present
+    // - gets its deltas summed into the SAME shared position, moving
+    // the cursor further/faster than the real physical motion and
+    // making the two clicks of a double-click land at inconsistently
+    // different positions, failing the file browser's own "same spot"
+    // check. Button/wheel/key events are NOT gated the same way here
+    // (those are boolean/discrete, not accumulative, so duplicate
+    // reports from multiple fds aren't the same class of problem).
+    int active_movement_pollIdx = -1;
+
     for (int pollIdx = 0; pollIdx < pollFdCount; pollIdx++)
     {
     int fd = pollFds[pollIdx];
@@ -2527,12 +2549,16 @@ static void PollMouseEvents(void)
         {
             if (event.code == REL_X)
             {
-                if (platform.cursorRelative)
+                if (active_movement_pollIdx == -1 || active_movement_pollIdx == pollIdx)
                 {
-                    CORE.Input.Mouse.currentPosition.x = event.value;
-                    CORE.Input.Mouse.previousPosition.x = 0.0f;
+                    active_movement_pollIdx = pollIdx;
+                    if (platform.cursorRelative)
+                    {
+                        CORE.Input.Mouse.currentPosition.x = event.value;
+                        CORE.Input.Mouse.previousPosition.x = 0.0f;
+                    }
+                    else CORE.Input.Mouse.currentPosition.x += event.value;
                 }
-                else CORE.Input.Mouse.currentPosition.x += event.value;
 
                 // NOTE: For DRM touchscreen, do not simulate touch from mouse movement
                 // CORE.Input.Touch.position[0].x = CORE.Input.Mouse.currentPosition.x;
@@ -2541,12 +2567,16 @@ static void PollMouseEvents(void)
 
             if (event.code == REL_Y)
             {
-                if (platform.cursorRelative)
+                if (active_movement_pollIdx == -1 || active_movement_pollIdx == pollIdx)
                 {
-                    CORE.Input.Mouse.currentPosition.y = event.value;
-                    CORE.Input.Mouse.previousPosition.y = 0.0f;
+                    active_movement_pollIdx = pollIdx;
+                    if (platform.cursorRelative)
+                    {
+                        CORE.Input.Mouse.currentPosition.y = event.value;
+                        CORE.Input.Mouse.previousPosition.y = 0.0f;
+                    }
+                    else CORE.Input.Mouse.currentPosition.y += event.value;
                 }
-                else CORE.Input.Mouse.currentPosition.y += event.value;
 
                 // NOTE: For DRM touchscreen, do not simulate touch from mouse movement
                 // CORE.Input.Touch.position[0].y = CORE.Input.Mouse.currentPosition.y;
